content.text:
Now engine. Add helper methods:
- `hasRedirect(side)`
- `atkForAttack(side, entry)`
- `canSpecialSummon(side, uid)` maybe returns bool/object.
- `specialSummon`.
- normalSummon reject special-only.
- damage redirect.
- attack use atkForAttack, indestructible.

Let's carefully edit engine.

First helpers after atkOf/defOf. Add:

```js
  attackAtkOf(side, entry) {
    if (!entry) return 0;
    let atk = this.atkOf(side, entry);
    if (entry.card.attackPenalty === 'releasedDef') atk -= entry.releasedDef || 0;
    return Math.max(0, atk);
  }

  hasDamageRedirect(side) {
    return this.ownMonsters(side).some(m => !m.entry.faceDown && m.entry.card.battle?.redirectDamage);
  }
```

Name `attackAtkOf` or `atkForAttack`. Use `atkForAttack`.

Also `canSpecialSummon(side, uid, releaseRef)`:
Return object `{ok, reason, release, zone}`? UI needs reason. We can implement:

```js
  specialReleaseOptions(side, card) {
    return this.ownMonsters(side);
  }

  canSpecialSummon(side, uid, releaseRef = null) {
    if (this.winner) return { ok:false, reason:'デュエルは終了しています' };
    if (this.turn !== side) return {ok:false, reason:'自分のターンではありません'};
    const card = this.hand[side].find(c=>c.uid===uid);
    if (!card) ...
    if (card.kind !== 'monster' || !card.specialSummon) ...
    const own = this.ownMonsters(side);
    const need = card.specialSummon.release || 1;
    if (own.length < need) ...
    if (!this.field[side].monster.some(z=>!z)) return ...
    if (releaseRef) { const e=this.entryAt(side, releaseRef.kind, releaseRef.idx); if(!e) ... }
    return {ok:true, card, need, own};
  }
```

But if releaseRef points to EMZ, `entryAt` works. Good.

`specialSummon(side, uid, zoneIdx, releaseRef = null, ev=[])`:
- call canSpecialSummon.
- zone valid.
- release selection: if releaseRef provided find. Else choose lowest DEF (auto fallback).
- compute releasedDef = this.defOf(side, rel.entry).
- release: use `this.destroyEntry(side, rel.kind, rel.idx, ev, 'release')` then log. But destroyEntry pushes event type destroy and reason release. FX will animate destroy. Good. But maybe rename event type to 'release'? We can create `releaseEntry` to push `{type:'release',...}`. But fx doesn't handle release. Could add. Simpler use destroyEntry and log "リリース". The card goes to GY. Requirement uses リリース, not destroy; event reason makes clear. Add a new method:

```js
releaseEntry(side, kind, idx, ev = []) {
  const entry = this.entryAt(...);
  if (!entry) return null;
  ... clear zone, gy.push
  ev.push({type:'release', side, kind, idx, card, uid, def: this.defOf(...)});
  return entry;
}
```
But fx must handle release; add same case as destroy maybe. And card detail. This is cleaner. However, `destroyEntry` already does what we need. We'll just use it with reason 'release' and add log. Event type destroy might be semantically off but visually correct. To minimize changes, use it. Maybe update event type after? No.

- Move card from hand, create entry with `releasedDef`.
- `position: 'atk'` because special summon in atk.
- Does it consume normal summon? No.
- `ev.push({type:'specialSummon', ...})`.
- `this.checkWin(ev)` (release itself doesn't cause win, but okay).
- Trigger summon effects? VD11 has no `effect`; but generic support.
- Should special summon be limited once per turn? Not specified. Allow. But if multiple copies, can summon each by releasing. Fine.

`normalSummon` check:
```js
if (card.specialSummon) throw new DuelError('このカードはリリースによる特殊召喚でのみ出せます');
```

`damage` change:
```js
  damage(side, amount, ev = [], reason = 'damage') {
    const n = ...
    if (!n || this.winner) return;
    let target = side;
    if (this.hasDamageRedirect(side)) {
      target = other(side);
      ev.push({ type:'redirect', from: side, to: target, amount:n, reason });
      this.log(`${this.names[side]}へのダメージを${this.names[target]}へ転嫁（${n}）`, 'trap');
    }
    this.lp[target] = ...
    ev.push({ type:'damage', side: target, amount:n, lp:..., reason, redirected: target !== side });
    this.log(`${this.names[target]} に ${n} ダメージ...`, 'damage');
    this.checkWin(ev);
  }
```
Potential issue: `hasDamageRedirect(side)` checks own monsters including the VD11 itself. If VD11 is being destroyed simultaneously? Damage applied before destruction in attack, so fine. If VD11 is destroyed by effect and the effect also burns? resolveOps order matters; if burn after destroy no redirect. Good.

But if side has VD11 and damage is redirected, and target opponent also has VD11, no recursive redirect. Fine.

`attack`:
- direct: `const atk = this.atkForAttack(side, entry);` log includes penalty maybe:
  - if penalty > 0, log `（リリースしたモンスターのDEF ${penalty} 分低下）`.
  - event `baseAtk: this.atkOf(side, entry), atkPenalty`.
- monster battle: `const atk = this.atkForAttack(side, entry);`
- determine destruction then apply indestructibility:
```js
    const attackerImmune = !!entry.card.battle?.indestructible;
    const defenderImmune = !!dentry.card.battle?.indestructible;
    if (attackerImmune) destroyAttacker = false;
    if (defenderImmune) destroyTarget = false;
```
Need to make sure damage calculation still uses original destroyAttacker/destroyTarget to decide who takes damage. In current code, damage application uses `if (destroyAttacker) damage(side, damage) else damage(enemy, damage)`. If we set destroyAttacker=false due to immunity before damage, then damage would wrongly go to enemy. So compute damage recipient before immunity, then immunity only affects destruction. Use separate `battleLoser` or `damageSide`:
```js
let damageSide = null;
...
if (damage > 0) damageSide = destroyAttacker ? side : enemy;
...
const attackerImmune = ...
if (attackerImmune) destroyAttacker = false;
...
if (damage > 0 && damageSide) this.damage(damageSide, damage, ev, 'battle');
```
But in defense position, if atk < targetDef, `destroyAttacker = true; damage = targetDef - atk`; damage to attacker. If attacker immune, still damage to attacker (redirected). Good. If atk > targetDef, destroyTarget true, damage 0. If target immune, no destroy, damage 0. Fine.
- Equal ATK: both destroyed; if defender immune, only attacker destroyed; if attacker immune, only defender destroyed; if both immune, none. damage 0.
- Event add `attackerImmune`, `defenderImmune`, `baseAtk`, `atkPenalty`.
- If both destroyed flags false after immunity, log "戦闘では破壊されなかった". Good.
- Maybe log immunity: `if (attackerImmune && wouldDestroyAttacker) log(`${entry.card.name} は戦闘で破壊されない`, 'buff')`. But if damage redirect, perhaps. Add.

Also check `afterAttack`: triggers attack effects; if attacker destroyed but indestructible, still there. Good.

`endTurn` resets flags; no special flag.

Damage redirection for effect damage could cause weird game states: If you control VD11 and activate `greed` selfDamage, it redirects to opponent. That's per text. Good. If opponent controls VD11 and you burn them, redirects to you. Good.

But potential immediate win: `damage` triggers checkWin within, possibly winner. Then attack continues; code after `this.damage` may destroy cards etc. Existing behavior had same issue. After damage, if winner, `destroyEntry` still happens and log. Should be okay, but `this.checkWin` already. Might guard after damage? Not necessary. But for redirect event animation.

Now UI changes.

